'''Seto Prefecture''' ({{lang|ja|瀬戸県}}, ''Seto-ken'') is a [[Prefectures of Okaiken|prefecture]] of [[Okaiken]] located on the northern coast of the island of [[Yagikoshu]], facing the [[Katsuran Strait]] and the [[Katsuran Sea]]. With a population of 2,644,113 as of 2026, it is the third-most-populous prefecture on Yagikoshu after [[Toyozawa Metropolis]] and [[Mito Prefecture]]. The prefectural capital is the city of [[Minatojima]], a major port and transportation hub situated at one of the narrowest points of the Katsuran Strait.

Seto Prefecture is one of Okaiken's primary shipbuilding and maritime logistics centres. The [[Minatojima Shipyard]] is one of the nation's three major shipbuilding complexes, specialising in LNG carriers, container vessels, and autonomous cargo ships for the Katsuran Sea trade routes. The prefecture is also known for its distinctive agricultural product—the {{nihongo|''Seto onion''|瀬戸玉ねぎ|Seto-tamanegi}}, a sweet, mild onion variety that accounts for approximately 40% of Okaiken's domestic onion production.

The prefecture's position at the northern edge of Yagikoshu, where the island is separated from [[Ishijima]] by the narrow, turbulent [[Katsuran Strait]], has shaped its history and economy. The strait's powerful tidal currents—among the strongest in Adonia—made the area a notorious haven for pirates during the Sengoku period, and the subsequent establishment of the [[Seto Domain]] under the [[Honda Shogunate]] was explicitly intended to secure the maritime passage. Today, the [[Minatojima Tunnel]] carries the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] and the National Expressway under the strait, connecting Yagikoshu with Ishijima and making Seto Prefecture a critical node in the national transportation network.

== History ==

=== The pirate era and the Katsuran Strait ===
{{Main|Minatojima#History}}
During the turbulent [[Sengoku Period]] (1467–1582), the Katsuran Strait emerged as one of the most dangerous waterways in Okaiken. The strait's powerful tidal currents—reaching speeds of up to 15 km/h during spring tides—and its complex network of hidden reefs and rocky outcrops made it ideal territory for pirate fleets that preyed on merchant vessels attempting the passage between the western and eastern seas.

The area that is now Seto Prefecture was controlled by a loose confederation of pirate clans known as the {{nihongo|''Kaizoku League''|海賊連合|Kaizoku Rengō}}, who operated from hidden coves and fortified islands throughout the strait. The League's dominance was finally broken in 1568, when the warlord [[Ogasawara Terumasa]] dispatched a naval expedition under his general [[Yamamoto Kagenaka]] to clear the strait and secure the vital trade route.

=== The Seto Domain ===
Following the [[Battle of Tone]] in 1600, the victorious [[Honda Shigetada]] (later [[Emperor Shirakawa of Mitoyagi|Emperor Shirakawa]]) recognised the enduring strategic value of the Katsuran Strait. He granted the area to {{nihongo|Koyama Naomori|小山直盛}} (1545–1623), one of his most trusted generals, with explicit instructions to maintain a permanent naval presence and prevent the resurgence of piracy.

The Koyama clan governed the {{nihongo|'''Seto Domain'''|瀬戸藩|Seto-han}} for over 250 years, transforming the small fishing village of Minatojima into a prosperous commercial centre. The domain's prosperity was built on three foundations: the maritime trade through the strait, the fertile agricultural lands of the coastal plain, and the distinctive sweet onion variety that became the domain's signature agricultural product.

=== Modern industrial development ===
The [[Saito Restoration]] of 1871 transformed Minatojima from a feudal domain into a modern industrial port. The city was officially incorporated on April 1, 1889, and the following decades saw the development of the shipbuilding and heavy industry that would define the prefecture's modern economy.

During the [[Great Adonian War]] (1947–1952), Minatojima became a primary target for the [[Illypnian Coalition]] due to its naval shipyards and its critical role as a logistics hub. The city suffered significant damage, but post-war reconstruction under Governor [[Takeda Mitsuo]] rebuilt and expanded the industrial infrastructure.

The construction of the [[Minatojima Tunnel]] (1978–1985)—a monumental engineering project carrying the [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] and National Expressway 5 under the Katsuran Strait—revolutionised the prefecture's transportation infrastructure and cemented Seto's status as a critical national hub.

== Geography ==

Seto Prefecture occupies the northern coast of [[Yagikoshu]] island, where the land meets the [[Katsuran Strait]]—the narrow, turbulent passage that separates Yagikoshu from [[Ishijima]] to the north. The prefecture is bounded by the [[Suzuka Mountains]] to the south, [[Toyozawa Metropolis]] to the west, and [[Kurosaki Prefecture]] to the east.

The prefecture's coastline is defined by the dramatic tidal dynamics of the Katsuran Strait. The funnel shape of the strait accelerates tidal currents to speeds exceeding 15 km/h during spring tides, creating dangerous but navigable conditions that have shaped the region's maritime culture for centuries.

The [[Suzuka River]] flows through the prefecture from its source in the Suzuka Mountains, emptying into the Katsuran Sea at Minatojima. The river's alluvial plain supports the prefecture's intensive agriculture, particularly the famous Seto onion fields.

