== Economy ==

Seto Prefecture's economy is built on three pillars: shipbuilding and maritime logistics, agriculture (particularly onion production), and technology services.

=== Shipbuilding and maritime logistics ===
{{Main|Minatojima#Economy}}
The [[Minatojima Shipyard]], operated by [[Minato Heavy Industries]], is one of Okaiken's three primary shipbuilding complexes alongside the [[Aizawa]] shipyards on Ishijima and the [[Sangoku Heavy Industries]] Toyozawa shipyard. The Minatojima facility specialises in LNG carriers for the Katsuran Sea trade routes, container vessels, and autonomous cargo ships. The shipyard employs approximately 7,800 workers and has an annual production capacity of 14 large vessels.

The [[Port of Minatojima]] is the third-busiest in Okaiken by cargo tonnage, handling approximately 68 million tonnes annually. The port's strategic position at the northern entrance to the Katsuran Strait makes it a critical transshipment hub for goods moving between the western and eastern seas.

=== The Seto onion ===
[[File:Seto Onion Fields.jpg|thumb|left|250px|Seto onion fields, producing approximately 40% of Okaiken's domestic onions]]
The {{nihongo|'''Seto onion'''|瀬戸玉ねぎ|Seto-tamanegi}} is one of Okaiken's most distinctive agricultural products. Grown in the fertile alluvial soils of the Suzuka River floodplain, the Seto onion is known for its exceptional sweetness, mild flavour, and high water content—characteristics derived from the unique combination of volcanic soil, coastal climate, and the traditional cultivation methods developed over centuries.

Seto Prefecture produces approximately 680,000 tonnes of onions annually, accounting for roughly 40% of Okaiken's domestic onion production. The onion is protected by a geographical indication (GI) designation, and the {{nihongo|Seto Onion Cooperative|瀬戸玉ねぎ協同組合|Seto-tamanegi Kyōdō Kumiai}} oversees quality standards and marketing. The annual {{nihongo|Seto Onion Festival|瀬戸玉ねぎ祭り|Seto-tamanegi Matsuri}}, held in Minatojima each June, draws over 150,000 visitors.

=== The Suzuka Data Corridor ===
The {{nihongo|'''Suzuka Data Corridor'''|鈴鹿データ回廊|Suzuka dēta-kairō}} is a regional development initiative designed to attract technology companies to the prefecture. Building on the connectivity advantages provided by the Minatojima Tunnel and the availability of former industrial land, the Corridor has attracted numerous software development firms, data centre operators, and IT services companies. The [[Digital Harbor]] development on the reclaimed Minatojima waterfront houses offices for over 80 technology companies.
Seto's strategic maritime position has made it a pioneer in next-generation subterranean freight logistics. Headquartered on the western waterfront, the [[Katsuran Subsea Transit Corporation]] operates the commercial pneumatic tube rail line passing beneath the Katsuran Strait, linking Seto with Toyozawa Metropolis. The system utilizes variable-buoyancy pods propelled by compressed air to shuttle high-value electronic components and temperature-sensitive mackerel cargo underwater, completely bypassing surface typhoon disruptions. The prefecture's coastal yards construct specialized container barges, while traditional food artisans maintain pressed mackerel curing cellars that supply gourmet markets throughout the Seto Inland Sea.

== Transportation ==

Seto Prefecture serves as a primary transportation hub for the connection between Yagikoshu and Ishijima.

=== Rail ===
[[File:Minatojima Tunnel Entrance.jpg|thumb|right|250px|The Minatojima Tunnel carrying the Ishijima Shinkansen under the Katsuran Strait]]
The [[Ishijima Shinkansen]] (Red Line) traverses the prefecture, with [[Sandaguchi Station (Okaiken)|Sandaguchi Station]] in [[Sanda, Seto|Sanda]] and [[Minatojima Station (Okaiken)|Minatojima Station]] in the prefectural capital serving as key stops connecting the prefecture directly to [[Toyozawa Central Station|Toyozawa]] to the south and [[Aizawa]] on Ishijima to the north. At the Katsuran Strait, the Shinkansen traverses the [[Minatojima Tunnel]]—a 14.7 km undersea tunnel that represents one of the most significant engineering achievements in Okaiken's history.

Regional and commuter rail connections in southern Seto Prefecture are anchored by Sanda and Osaki:
* '''[[Sankuro Railway]] (三黒鉄道, ''Sankuro''):''' The mountain-climbing commuter railway tackling steep grades across the foothills of Mount Hoshisasa, connecting [[Sanda, Seto|Sanda]] directly south to [[Toyozawa]] and east to [[Kurosaki]], while also operating a direct feeder line between Mito and Sanda.
* '''[[Osaki Railway]] (大崎鉄道):''' Running parallel to the Ishijima Shinkansen along the scenic coast of the Katsuran Sea, providing coastal commuter connections between the industrial port of [[Osaki, Seto|Osaki]] (population 121,405) and downtown Toyozawa.
* '''[[Toyokyu]] (Toyozawa Kyuko Railway / 豊急):''' Operates high-capacity commuter services along the Osaki Line into the elevated 9-platform terminal at Toyozawa Kita.
* '''[[OR Yagikoshu]] ([[Okaiken Railways Group]]):''' Conventional 1,067 mm Cape-gauge operations, including the [[OR Seto Main Line]] connecting Toyozawa through Sanda toward Osaki and Minatojima, and the OR Hoshisasa Line linking Sanda to [[Isshinji Central Station]]. The historic [[Suzuka Main Line]] connects regional agricultural and industrial centres across the prefecture.

=== Road ===
National Expressway 5 passes through the Minatojima Tunnel, providing road connectivity between Yagikoshu and Ishijima. The [[Seto Beltway]], completed in 2008, connects the harbour area to the southern industrial zones.

=== Ferry ===
The [[Minatojima Ferry Terminal]] provides regular services to [[Okami]] (6 hours), [[Aizawa]] (3 hours), and the [[Ogasawara Islands]] (18 hours).

== Culture ==

=== Cuisine ===
Seto Prefecture's cuisine reflects its maritime heritage and its agricultural traditions. The most distinctive local specialties include:

* {{nihongo|''Minato-don''|港丼}} — a bowl of rice topped with raw coastal fish cured in coconut vinegar and ginger, reflecting the city's historical trade connections with ports across the Shendan Ocean
* {{nihongo|''Seto onion dishes''|瀬戸玉ねぎ料理}} — the sweet Seto onion is used in numerous preparations, from simple grilled onions to complex sauces for seafood
* {{nihongo|''Katsuran himono''|葛陸干物}} — dried fish prepared using the traditional tide-drying method

=== Festivals ===
The {{nihongo|'''Two Seas Festival'''|両海祭|Ryōkai Matsuri}}, held annually from July 15 to 17, commemorates Minatojima's role as the meeting point between the Shendan Ocean and the Katsuran Inland Sea. The festival originated as a religious observance in the 16th century and has evolved into a major cultural event featuring boat races, fireworks, and traditional performances.

